Hello Laura,

an editorial lead on a point that tends to be glossed over in this software
category: where a dimension comes from.

Solidon3D reads downloaded meshes and makes their features editable without
CAD. Click a hole, its dimensions appear on the model, type a new diameter and
the countersink follows, pick a fit for the screw, read the check report
before export. With version 0.5.0 two things changed that are relevant for a
manufacturing readership.

First, the result is an exact body. Holes, countersinks and threads stay real
faces and edges when they are moved, duplicated or removed, and STEP goes in
and comes back out cleanly. Two geometry kernels run under one interface, and
the options that used to expose that choice have been removed; the software
keeps the exact body where it can and names the limit where it cannot, rather
than converting silently.

Second, and this is the part I would offer as the angle: every dimension
states its provenance. The feature window says whether a value was measured on
the mesh, fitted to a surface, or taken from the construction step that
created it. For anyone documenting a part, that distinction is the difference
between a number and a specification. In the same spirit, a recognised feature
keeps its identity through edits. Closing a hole and drilling a new one
produces identical geometry but a different feature, and any fit pointing at
it loses its reference, while moving it does not.

The same thinking runs through the tolerances. A clearance is not a number in
the model but a reference to the material, and the material profile can be
calibrated against printed test pieces, so a documented fit carries the
printer it was verified on rather than a value someone remembered.

Other items: a honeycomb pattern or knurl is recognised as one feature with
pitch, cell width and depth; an imported thread is measured, so pitch, starts,
handedness and nominal diameter, and can have its counterpart generated in a
table size as a fit; resin printers are supported with pixel size and minimum
wall; fits are checked on the real bodies in their assembled position.

Solidon3D is an independent development from Germany, desktop, fully usable
offline after a single activation. The demo is free and complete, so any of
the above can be checked directly.

